CAT = "sqeLegalServices"; SEG = "LSV"
Q = [
("Two clients are bidding for the same business and both give informed written consent. Can one firm act for both?",
 "Yes, with safeguards", ["No, it is always banned", "Yes, with no safeguards", "Only if one is a former client"],
 "Paragraph 6.2 of the Code allows acting in a client conflict where clients compete for the same objective, all give informed written consent, effective safeguards protect confidentiality and it is reasonable to act."),
("A client wants to buy a flat the solicitor personally owns. Can the solicitor act for the client?",
 "No, as her own interest conflicts", ["Yes, with written consent", "Yes, if the price is fair", "Only on a fixed fee"],
 "Paragraph 6.1 forbids acting where there is an own interest conflict or significant risk of one, and no consent exception applies."),
("A client's complaint to the firm is unresolved. After how long can she take it to the Legal Ombudsman?",
 "Eight weeks", ["Four weeks", "Twelve weeks", "Six months"],
 "Paragraph 8.4 requires clients to be told in writing of their right to complain to the Legal Ombudsman if the complaint is not resolved within eight weeks."),
("A solicitor gives an undertaking to another firm on a sale. Who must make sure it is performed?",
 "The solicitor", ["The client alone", "The firm's COFA only", "No one, unless by deed"],
 "Paragraph 1.3 requires a solicitor to perform all undertakings within an agreed or reasonable time; the solicitor is personally answerable and the SRA treats breach as misconduct."),
("A client admits the theft to you but insists on pleading not guilty. What may you do?",
 "Test the prosecution case", ["Call him to deny the theft", "Tell the court he admitted it", "Run an alibi you think false"],
 "You must not mislead the court (paragraph 1.4), so you cannot put forward a positive false case, but you may put the prosecution to proof and test its evidence."),
("A solicitor suspects funds a client is sending are criminal proceeds. To whom should she report within the firm?",
 "The nominated officer (MLRO)", ["The client's own bank", "The compliance officer (COFA)", "The Legal Ombudsman"],
 "Under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, an internal report to the firm's nominated officer (the MLRO) is a protected disclosure; the officer decides whether to report to the NCA."),
("A client seeking legal advice tells his solicitor facts suggesting past money laundering. Must she report it?",
 "No, privileged circumstances", ["Yes, at once to the NCA", "Yes, after warning the client", "Only above a set sum"],
 "POCA s.330(6) and (10) exempt a professional legal adviser from the duty to disclose information received in privileged circumstances, unless it is given to further a criminal purpose."),
("A firm wants to cold-call people injured in a recent crash to offer its services. Is that allowed?",
 "No, it is an unsolicited approach", ["Yes, if the calls are polite", "Yes, after a week has passed", "Only by a trainee solicitor"],
 "Paragraph 8.9 of the Code bars unsolicited approaches to members of the public to advertise services, except to current or former clients."),
("When must a solicitor give a client the best possible information about likely costs?",
 "At engagement and later", ["Only when the final bill is sent", "Only if the client asks", "Only for fixed fee work"],
 "Paragraph 8.7 requires the best possible information on pricing and likely overall cost at the time of engagement and when appropriate as the matter progresses."),
("A client wants a series of aggressive letters to stop a journalist publishing a fair, accurate story. What is the risk?",
 "Abusive SLAPP conduct", ["A routine pre-action step", "A legitimate claim in privacy law", "A normal letter of claim"],
 "The SRA warning notice on SLAPPs says using litigation threats to stifle legitimate public interest reporting can breach the Principles and Code, including acting with integrity."),
("A settlement agreement would stop an employee reporting misconduct to the police. Is that clause proper?",
 "No, it cannot stop that", ["Yes, if both sides sign", "Yes, if paid generously", "Only if it is secret"],
 "The SRA warning notice on NDAs says agreements must not be used to prevent reports to the police, a regulator or other law enforcement body."),
("A claims company offers to send the firm personal injury clients for a fee per client. May the firm pay it?",
 "No, they are banned", ["Yes, if it is disclosed", "Yes, if it is reasonable", "Only for road traffic accident cases"],
 "Section 56 Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 bans referral fees in personal injury cases, whatever disclosure is made."),
]
